
THE DEFINITIVE DEVOPS INTERVIEW HANDBOOK
Company-Wise and Topic-Wise Preparation, Built From Real Interview Data
1,307 unique canonical questions · 1,390 recorded occurrences · 38 named companies · 32 technology topics
No answers included — this is a pure interview-question reference.
How to Use This Handbook
This handbook slices the same underlying question repository along two independent axes, so you can prepare either by company or by technology without losing your place.
Part 1 — Company-Wise Preparation
One chapter per company. Every question ever recorded against that company appears in its chapter, grouped by topic — including duplicates, because a candidate preparing for a specific company wants that company's full history, even if another company asked the same thing. Each chapter opens with a quick statistics summary and closes with a one-page revision sheet.
Part 2 — Master Topic Repository
This is the primary learning section. One chapter per technology. Every canonical question appears exactly once, deduplicated across companies. Each entry lists which companies asked it, any alternative interview wordings recorded for it, and related questions from the same topic so you can build a natural study path. Each topic chapter closes with a Quick Revision Checklist.
Part 3 — Most Frequently Asked Questions
Questions that were independently asked by two or more named companies, ranked by how many companies asked them. This is a strong signal of what's worth prioritizing — the ranking uses only what's in the data; nothing here is estimated or invented.
Part 4 — Ultimate DevOps Revision Sheet
Every unique canonical question, one line each, organized by topic, with no duplicates — built for last-day cramming.
A note on scope: difficulty tiers have been deliberately removed throughout — real interviews don't announce a question's difficulty in advance, so this handbook doesn't either. Only questions and companies actually present in the source repository are included; nothing has been invented. This edition merges two source handbooks into one unified reference; where the same company appeared in both, its question sets were combined and de-duplicated.
 
